% Display the results
disp(['The value of x is: ', num2str(x_val)]);
```

This code will output the value of 'x' based on the given system of equations.

Here's a simple SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity-Check) encoder for an FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] data_reg;
reg [3:0] parity_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        parity_reg <= 4'b0000;
    end else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= parity_calc(data_reg);
    end
end

function [4:0] parity_calc(data);
    // Implementation of parity check calculation goes here
    // Return the parity bits as a 4-bit vector
endfunction

assign data_out = data_reg | parity_reg;

endmodule
```

This module has a clock input (clk), a reset input (reset), and two data inputs (data_in and data_out). The data_in signal is used to feed the data to be encoded, while the data_out signal is used to output the encoded data along with the parity bits.

The internal signals include a 8-bit data register (data_reg) and a 4-bit parity register (parity_reg). The data_reg is updated with the incoming data on each clock cycle, while the parity_reg is updated with the result of the parity calculation.

The parity calculation function (parity_calc) takes an 8-bit data vector as input and returns a 4-bit parity vector as output. You can implement the parity calculation logic according to your desired LDPC encoding scheme.

Finally, the data_out signal is assigned the value of the data_reg concatenated with the parity_reg. This ensures that the encoded data is outputted along with the parity bits.

Yes, you can achieve this by using Jira's built-in automation rules and the Checklist for Jira plugin. Here's how:

1. Install the Checklist for Jira plugin if you haven't already.
2. Create a new checklist for the issue type(s) you want to apply this rule to.
3. In your Jira board's workflow, create a new transition between the columns you want to block issues from moving to.
4. Add a post-function to the transition that will check if all items in the checklist are marked as done. You can do this by using the `checklist.allDone()` method in the Checklist for Jira plugin.
5. If all items in the checklist are marked as done, prevent the issue from moving to the next column by adding a condition to the transition that checks if the checklist is complete. You can do this by using the `checklist.isComplete()` method in the Checklist for Jira plugin.
6. If the checklist is not complete, allow the issue to move to the next column as normal.

Here's an example of how the post-function might look:
```javascript
function checklistIsComplete(issue) {
    const checklist = issue.fields.checklist;
    return checklist.allDone() && checklist.isComplete();
}

function preventMovement(issue) {
    const transition = issue.transitionTo(nextColumn);
    transition.addCondition({
        name: 'Checklist Complete',
        expression: checklistIsComplete(issue),
        description: 'Prevent movement if all items in the checklist are marked as done.'
    });
}
```
Replace `nextColumn` with the name of the column you want to block issues from moving to.

This way, when an issue is moved to the transition, Jira will check if all items in the checklist are marked as done. If they are, the issue will be prevented from moving to the next column. If they are not, the issue will be allowed to move to the next column as normal.

