Here's a simple After Effects script to create a vertical video by stacking two other videos vertically, adding subtitles to the top half of the video, and using a text file as a source for the subtitles:

```javascript
function main() {
  var project = app.project;
  var comp = project.activeItem;

  // Set up variables for the input files and output file
  var videoFile1Path = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\video1.mp4";
  var videoFile2Path = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\video2.mp4";
  var subtitleTextFile = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\subtitles.txt";
  var outputFilePath = "C:\\Users\\UserName\\Desktop\\output.mp4";

  // Create a new composition
  var newComp = project.createComposition(640, 1080, 30);

  // Import the first video file into the new composition
  var video1Layer = newComp.layers.add(Import.file(videoFile1Path));
  video1Layer.name = "Video 1";

  // Import the second video file into the new composition
  var video2Layer = newComp.layers.add(Import.file(videoFile2Path));
  video2Layer.name = "Video 2";

  // Stack the two video layers vertically
  video1Layer.moveToBack();
  video2Layer.moveToFront();

  // Add subtitles to the top half of the video
  var subtitleLayer = newComp.layers.add(Subtitle.text(subtitleTextFile));
  subtitleLayer.name = "Subtitles";
  subtitleLayer.position = [0, 0];
  subtitleLayer.scale = [1, 0.5];

  // Export the final video
  Export.render(newComp, outputFilePath);
}

main();
```

Make sure to replace the file paths with the actual paths to your video files and subtitle text file. Also, make sure to adjust the position and scale of the subtitle layer to your liking.

To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Next.js when using WordPress as the backend, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project:
```bash
npm install cors
```
or
```bash
yarn add cors
```
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages/api` directory.
3. Add the following code to the `cors.js` file:
```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com', // Replace with the URL of your WordPress site
});

export default async (req, res) => {
  await cors(req, res);

  // Your Next.js API route goes here
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site.

4. In your Next.js app, import the `cors` function and use it in your API routes. For example:
```javascript
import { cors } from '../../pages/api/cors';

export default async (req, res) => {
  await cors(req, res);

  // Your Next.js API route goes here
};
```
5. Save your changes and restart your Next.js development server.

Now, when you make requests from your Next.js app to your WordPress site, the CORS error should be fixed.

