Here's a simple example of a toast notification using React Context:

```jsx
import React, { createContext, useEffect, useState } from 'react';

const ToastContext = createContext();

function ToastProvider({ children }) {
  const [toasts, setToasts] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
      setToasts(prevToasts => prevToasts.filter(toast => toast.duration > Date.now()));
    }, 3000);

    return () => clearTimeout(timeout);
  }, [toasts]);

  const addToast = (text, duration) => {
    setToasts(prevToasts => [...prevToasts, { text, duration }]);
  };

  return (
    <ToastContext.Provider value={{ addToast }}>
      {children}
    </ToastContext.Provider>
  );
}

function ToastConsumer() {
  const { addToast } = React.useContext(ToastContext);

  const handleClick = () => {
    addToast('This is a toast notification!', 5000);
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={handleClick}>Add Toast</button>
  );
}

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <ToastProvider>
        <ToastConsumer />
      </ToastProvider>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this example, we have a `ToastProvider` component that wraps the application and provides a `ToastContext` object with an `addToast` function. The `ToastConsumer` component consumes the `ToastContext` object and renders a button that adds a toast notification when clicked.

The toast notifications are displayed for 3 seconds before being automatically removed. You can adjust the duration by passing a different value to the `addToast` function.

