To accomplish this task, follow these steps:

1. Install Accord.NET NuGet package.
2. Load the dataset into a DataTable with columns for sentence text and category.
3. Create a NaiveBayes classifier and fit it to the dataset using the CountVectorizer class.
4. Use the fitted classifier to predict the labels for new sentences.
5. Demonstrate the classification process using a sample sentence and output the predicted label.

Here's a concise and elegant C# code that accomplishes this task:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Classification;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Vectorization;
using Accord.Math;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Load the dataset into a DataTable
        var dataset = new DataTable();
        dataset.Columns.Add("Sentence", typeof(string));
        dataset.Columns.Add("Category", typeof(string));

        // Add your dataset rows here

        // Create a NaiveBayes classifier and fit it to the dataset
        var classifier = new NaiveBayes();
        var vectorizer = new CountVectorizer();
        var transformedDataset = vectorizer.Transform(dataset);
        classifier.Fit(transformedDataset);

        // Use the fitted classifier to predict the labels for new sentences
        string newSentence = "The sports game was exciting";
        var newRow = new DataRow(newSentence, "Unknown");
        var transformedNewRow = vectorizer.Transform(newRow);
        var prediction = classifier.Predict(transformedNewRow);

        // Output the predicted label
        Console.WriteLine($"Predicted label: {prediction}");
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace the `// Add your dataset rows here` comment with your actual dataset.

