To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple collision detection system. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function that checks for collisions before adding a new planet:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this version, we check if the new planet is too close to any existing planet by iterating over the existing planets and checking if the distance between their positions is less than the minimum distance. If it is, we skip adding the new planet and try again with a new position.

You can adjust the `minDistance` variable to control the maximum distance between the planets. A smaller value will result in more tightly packed planets, while a larger value will give them more space.

